package Vixen.Shaders.PostFx

import Vixen.Shaders.Core
import Vixen.Shaders.Geometry
import Vixen.Shaders.Shading

// Putting a split frame back together: the other half of `ForwardPlus.SplitOutputs`.
//
// A split pass writes direct light to one target and holds the diffuse ambient back, because ambient
// is the term the screen-space passes have something to say about — an irradiance plane knows what
// light arrives, an occlusion plane knows how much of it survives, and neither can reach a term that
// was summed into one number a pass ago. This is where that term is finally applied:
//
//     combined = direct × sunVisibility + albedo × irradiance × occlusion, reflections blended over
//
// where `irradiance` is a screen plane if the frame has one and the scene's own sky if it does not —
// see the ⚠ two paragraphs down, which is the difference between a split frame and a dark one.
//
// Every plane after the first three is optional, and each has a stand-in with stated semantics: the
// host binds *something* into every slot — a set is written wholly or not at all — and a `use…`
// switch at zero makes the term read as "off" (occlusion 1, sun 1, reflections 0) whatever texels
// the stand-in holds. A document that names no AO node gets unoccluded ambient, not a frame
// modulated by garbage.
//
// ⚠ **Irradiance is the one plane whose absence is not "off", because "off" was a black hole.** The
// split pass withholds the diffuse ambient unconditionally and this node is the only thing that can
// put it back; an absent irradiance plane therefore used to mean the term was withheld and never
// restored. `!StandardFrame` splits for reflections as well as for GI, so the two configurations
// that hit it — `gi: ambient` and `gi: off, reflections: screen` — were whole frames rendered with
// no diffuse ambient in them at all. So the stand-in for irradiance is the scene's own sky,
// `environmentSh`, evaluated per pixel against the normals plane: the same nine coefficients and
// the same `Ibl.IrradianceSh9` the unsplit pass reads, which is what makes splitting a frame with
// no GI in it change nothing. `useEnvironmentSh` at zero is the honest nothing, for a scene that
// has no environment.
//
// ⚠ **Sun visibility multiplies DIRECT and occlusion multiplies AMBIENT — `!DistanceFieldAo`'s own
// rule, kept here.** Its plane carries occlusion in `r` and sun visibility in `g` precisely so a
// consumer can multiply them into different terms; pre-combining them into one darkness is the
// classic mistake that makes a scene look dirty rather than grounded. And where a shadow map already
// shadows the sun, the document sets that node's `sunShadow: false` — its `g` then stays 1 and
// nothing is applied twice. The double-application guard is the AO node's knob, not arithmetic here.
//
// The sun factor reaches emissive too, because target 0 carries direct and emissive as one number.
// That is a knowing approximation: the sun channel is a soft wide term, and a frame that cannot
// accept it shadows the sun through the cascades and turns the channel off, as above.
//
// The AO pair usually arrives at half resolution, and this is the one place it meets the full-res
// frame — so the upsample lives here rather than in a pass of its own. With a depth bound, each AO
// plane is read through `Upsample` below: four texels, bilinear coverage, and the probe upsample's
// plane test, which is what keeps half-res occlusion from smearing across the depth edge at a
// corner and reading as occlusion that stands beside the crease that earned it.
//
// ⚠ **The reflections plane's alpha is VALIDITY, not reflectance, and blending by it alone made
// every surface a mirror.** `ReflectionTrace` writes a literal 1 into alpha at every pixel that has
// a surface — the flag means "the trace answered here", and nothing about it says how much of that
// radiance a surface actually sends to the eye. Lerping by it therefore *replaced* the whole
// combined colour at every shaded pixel: the direct sun term, the rebuilt diffuse ambient and so
// the albedo, all of it, with the traced radiance. On sample 13's concrete (roughness 0.81,
// metalness 0) that rendered every wall as a flat mirror of the irradiance field, pixel-identical
// whether the material's base colour was red or black.
//
// So the weight is the surface's own specular reflectance, computed here from the planes this pass
// already reads: `Ibl.EnvironmentDfg` against the normals plane's roughness and the view angle,
// times a dielectric `f0`. That is the same split-sum term `Ibl.Specular` applies to a prefiltered
// cube, which is what makes the lerp read as *replacing* the pass's own IBL specular with the
// traced answer rather than as painting over the frame — and what keeps a rough dielectric at the
// four per cent it physically reflects.
//
// ⚠ **The weighting belongs to the consumer, which is why it is here and not in the trace's alpha.**
// `Water.rvn` reads the same plane and multiplies it by *water's* Fresnel against its own `f0` —
// a reflectance baked into the producer would be applied twice there, at the wrong index. One
// plane, one meaning (radiance and validity), and each consumer weighs it by the BRDF it has.
//
// ⚠ **`f0` comes from a plane of its own — the split's fourth target — and it took a fourth target
// because nothing smaller carries it.** Before that plane existed this pass assumed a dielectric
// 0.04 for every surface, so a metal's traced reflection was weighted here as though it were paint:
// too dim, and dimmer the more metallic it is. The stand-in rule still gives that answer for a
// document that binds no specular plane (`useSpecular` at zero), and it is still a floor rather
// than an estimate.
//
// **What that plane had to be, audited rather than assumed.** The obvious reading is "one
// metalness channel", and it is wrong twice over:
//
//   - **`MaterialData` has no metalness and cannot be asked for one.** It keeps `diffuseColor` and
//     `f0`, which is what a BRDF wants, and the pair is *underdetermined*: `diffuse = base(1 - m)`
//     and `f0 = lerp(0.04, base, m)` are two equations in three unknowns. `GBuffer.Metalness`
//     claims to invert them and is exact only at the ends — a base of 0.8 at `m = 0.5` gives
//     `(0.42 - 0.04) / (0.40 - 0.04) = 1.06`, saturating to 1, a half-metal encoded as a mirror.
//     And a metalness channel does not help even where it is right, because the albedo plane holds
//     `base(1 - m)`, which is *black* at `m = 1`: there is no base colour left to rebuild `f0` from.
//   - So the plane has to carry **`f0` itself, three channels**. That is what settled the choice
//     between a fourth target and repacking `SceneNormals` octahedrally: octahedral folds `xyz`
//     into `xy` and frees **one** channel, not the three this needs. It also cannot be done at all
//     without a new validity signal, because `Math.EncodeOctahedral(0, 0, 1)` is `(0, 0)` — exactly
//     the cleared sky — and the length test twelve lines below `Fragment` is the only sky test this
//     pass has.
//
// ⚠ **And the fourth plane could not land alone, which is why the blend below adds.** With a real
// `f0` a lerp removes `reflectance ×` the whole combined colour, and on a metal `reflectance` is
// around 0.55 rather than 0.04 — so it would take away half of the surface's *direct* sunlight to
// make room for the traced answer. Correcting `f0` therefore forced the other half of the same
// change: the split pass stops writing `AmbientSpecular` into target 0, and this **adds**
// `mirror × reflectance` rather than lerping toward it. One plane, one uniform on
// `ClusteredShading` (`ambientSpecularScale`), and `AmbientCombineRenderer.Build` turning both on
// together — a half of either is a frame that counts the sky twice or a metal that reflects
// nothing.
//
// ⚠ **It is a look decision for every surface in the frame and not for metals alone.** Dropping
// `AmbientSpecular` moves *every* surface's specular ambient from the prefiltered cube to whatever
// `ReflectionTrace` answers — and above its `roughnessThreshold` that answer is the irradiance
// field rather than a trace. Sample 13's concrete is roughness 0.81 against a threshold of 0.45, so
// the walls and the floor take that route too. The two are estimates of the same dusk sky and
// should land close; a large divergence on rough surfaces is a finding about one of them.
//
// The size of what was bought, measured on sample 13's ramp — the level's one metal — at 512
// frames. Before: zeroing `AmbientSpecular` moved that surface by (7.9, 8.3, 5.1) of
// (99.2, 81.0, 71.3), and zeroing this pass's reflection blend moved it by (0.9, 2.6, 2.9) — so the
// correction redirects about a twelfth of the metal's pixel between two estimates of the same dusk
// sky. Realised: (99.2, 81.0, 71.3) to (101.8, 83.6, 73.7), a twelfth's worth of term moving for a
// fortieth's worth of pixel, which is what it should be when the two estimates agree. A delta much
// *larger* than that twelfth would have meant they were being counted together, which is what the
// addition and the scalar exist to prevent.
//
// The rough dielectrics moved by half a level out of 255 — the concrete floor (roughness 0.81, so
// the irradiance field answers rather than a trace) went (60.5, 54.2, 51.3) to (61.0, 54.6, 51.6),
// and the whole frame's mean channel 102.633 to 102.721. That is the finding the paragraph above
// asks for: the prefiltered cube and `ReflectionTrace`'s wide answer are estimates of the same sky
// and they do not disagree. Too dim remains the safe direction to be wrong in.

shader AmbientCombine {
    /// The split pass's first target: direct light, emissive and specular ambient; alpha carried.
    var direct: Texture2D

    /// Its second: diffuse albedo in `rgb`, the material's own occlusion in `a` — 1 where it has none.
    var albedo: Texture2D

    /// Its third: world normal in `xyz`, roughness in `a`.
    ///
    /// A zero vector marks a pixel no surface wrote, which is this pass's sky test. The roughness
    /// used to be "the reflection passes' business" and is read here now: it is half of what
    /// `Reflectance` needs to weigh the traced plane by something physical.
    var normals: Texture2D

    /// Screen irradiance over π in `rgb` — what `!IndirectDiffuse` publishes, and what
    /// `!ScreenProbeGather`'s upsample publishes; either plane multiplies straight by albedo.
    /// Stand-in semantics: `useIrradiance` 0 falls back to `environmentSh` below — see it for why
    /// that is not the same as contributing nothing.
    var irradiance: Texture2D

    /// `!DistanceFieldAo`'s plane: occlusion in `r`, sun visibility in `g`.
    /// Stand-in semantics: `useOcclusion` 0 reads both as 1 — fully open, fully lit.
    var occlusion: Texture2D

    /// `!Ssao`'s plane: occlusion in `r`. A second, contact-scale term over the field's room-scale
    /// one. Stand-in semantics: `useContactOcclusion` 0 reads it as 1.
    var contactOcclusion: Texture2D

    /// `!Reflections`' plane: radiance in `rgb`, **validity** in `a` — not reflectance, see above.
    /// Stand-in semantics: `useReflections` 0 blends none of it in.
    var reflections: Texture2D

    /// The split pass's fourth target: specular reflectance at normal incidence in `rgb`.
    ///
    /// `ForwardTargets.specular`, `TerrainTargets.specular` and the resolve's fourth storage image
    /// all write the same thing — `MaterialData.f0` — and this is the only consumer of it.
    /// Stand-in semantics: `useSpecular` 0 reads a dielectric 0.04 for every pixel, which is the
    /// weight this pass used before the plane existed.
    var specular: Texture2D

    /// For the full-resolution planes the split pass wrote — texel-for-texel, nothing to filter.
    var pointSampler: Sampler

    /// For the planes that may be half resolution — the AO pair, irradiance, reflections.
    var linearSampler: Sampler

    /// The full-resolution depth, for the AO upsample below. Read only above zero `useBilateral`;
    /// the stand-in rule covers the binding, as it does every optional plane.
    var depthBuffer: Texture2D

    /// 1 where the named plane is real, 0 where the host bound a stand-in.
    ///
    /// Uniforms rather than permutations, deliberately: the bindings exist in every variant either
    /// way — a set is written wholly or not at all — so folding the reads would save four lerps and
    /// cost a variant per combination of inputs.
    var useIrradiance: float = 0f

    var useOcclusion: float = 0f

    var useContactOcclusion: float = 0f

    /// 1 where the traced plane *replaces* the split pass's own specular ambient, 0 otherwise.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **Not "is there a reflections resource".** The addition below only balances if the pass
    /// held its own specular ambient back, and it only holds it back if the host also zeroed
    /// `ClusteredShading.ambientSpecularScale`. One switch for both halves, set from one condition
    /// in `AmbientCombineRenderer.Build`, so the two can never disagree: on with only one of them,
    /// the frame either counts the dusk sky twice or loses a metal's reflection entirely.
    var useReflections: float = 0f

    /// 1 where `specular` is the real plane, 0 where the host bound a stand-in — see the binding.
    var useSpecular: float = 0f

    /// A multiplier on the rebuilt ambient alone: the **document's**, `!AmbientCombine`'s own
    /// `intensity:` knob, applied to whatever rebuilt the term — a screen plane or the sky below.
    ///
    /// ⚠ Not the scene's `ambientIntensity`, which arrives separately and under its own name now
    /// that this pass reads the environment. This used to be described as that value's split-mode
    /// seat, and it could not be: nothing wrote it from an `EnvironmentLight`, and one seat for the
    /// compositor's number and the scene's would have them overwriting each other.
    var intensity: float = 1f

    /// The scene's sky as nine coefficients: `EnvironmentLight.Irradiance`, the same projection
    /// `ClusteredShading.Ambient` reads under the same name.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **This is what a split frame with no irradiance plane is lit by, and without it there was
    /// nothing.** The split pass withholds diffuse ambient unconditionally, on the promise that this
    /// node rebuilds it — but the only thing it had to rebuild it *from* was a screen plane, and a
    /// frame below `gi: probes` publishes none. `!StandardFrame` splits for reflections too, so
    /// `gi: ambient` and `gi: off, reflections: screen` both used to arrive here with
    /// `useIrradiance` at zero and lose the term outright: `material.rgb * incoming` with `incoming`
    /// identically zero. The sky is not a screen plane and needs no producer — nine numbers the
    /// scene already has — so it answers where nothing else does.
    var environmentSh: ShCoefficients

    /// 1 where the host wrote a real sky into `environmentSh`, 0 where nothing did.
    ///
    /// The stand-in rule again, and it has to be a switch rather than "are the coefficients zero":
    /// an unlit scene and a scene nobody told this pass about are the same nine zeros, and the
    /// second is a bug worth being able to see. A scene with no environment at all keeps the frame
    /// it had.
    var useEnvironmentSh: float = 0f

    /// `EnvironmentLight.Intensity`, on the reconstructed sky alone — the same name and the same
    /// value `ClusteredShading` reads, written by the same `EnvironmentLight.Apply`.
    ///
    /// The name is the protocol: `Apply` qualifies its nine coefficients and this scalar by whatever
    /// pass it is given, so declaring them under the spellings the shading passes use is what lets
    /// one environment write itself into this pass without a second code path to drift from.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **Not `intensity` above.** That is the document's multiplier and this is the scene's, and
    /// the rebuilt sky needs this one to land where the unsplit pass's ambient did.
    var ambientIntensity: float = 1f

    /// 1 to upsample the AO pair bilaterally, 0 for the plain linear read.
    ///
    /// The AO planes come in at half resolution, and a linear tap of them at a depth edge averages
    /// the two surfaces that meet there — occlusion that visibly stands a texel or two off the
    /// corner that earned it. Above zero, each plane is read as its four nearest texels weighted by
    /// the plane test below instead. Zero when the host bound no depth, because the test would then
    /// be against a stand-in.
    var useBilateral: float = 0f

    /// What turns a depth back into the world position the plane test stands in, and what the
    /// reflection weight's view direction is unprojected from.
    var inverseViewProjection: mat4

    /// 1 where `inverseViewProjection` is a camera's, 0 where nothing drove it.
    ///
    /// The reflection weight needs a view angle — a dielectric reflects four per cent head-on and
    /// nearly everything at a grazing one — and the only camera this pass has is that matrix. An
    /// identity there is not a harmless approximation: unprojecting through it gives a direction
    /// that has nothing to do with the frame, and a nonsense grazing angle is a *full mirror*
    /// rather than a slightly wrong tint. So the stand-in rule again, one switch along: at zero
    /// the weight is taken at normal incidence, which is the modest end of the range and can never
    /// blow a surface out.
    var useView: float = 0f

    /// One texel of the `occlusion` plane in UV, locating its four nearest texel centres.
    var occlusionTexel: float2 = float2(0.001f, 0.001f)

    /// One texel of the `contactOcclusion` plane, which may be a different size.
    var contactTexel: float2 = float2(0.001f, 0.001f)

    /// How far off this pixel's plane a reduced texel's surface may stand and still be averaged
    /// in, in world units — `ScreenProbeUpsample.planeTolerance`'s number, doing the same job.
    var planeTolerance: float = 0.05f

    stream var uv: float2

    [VertexShader]
    [Semantic("SV_Position")]
    func Vertex([Semantic("SV_VertexID")] vertexIndex: int): float4 {
        uv = Fullscreen.Uv(vertexIndex)
        return Fullscreen.Position(vertexIndex)
    }

    /// One pixel of combined radiance, SceneHdr-shaped: linear, pre-tonemap, alpha carried through.
    [FragmentShader]
    [Semantic("SV_Target")]
    func Fragment(): float4 {
        val lit = direct.Sample(pointSampler, uv)
        val surface = normals.Sample(pointSampler, uv)

        // A pixel no surface wrote — the sky, or anything a later pass composites. The normals
        // plane clears to zero and every writer stores a unit vector, so length is the honest test;
        // depth would need a fourth mandatory plane for one comparison.
        if (dot(surface.xyz, surface.xyz) < 0.25f) {
            return lit
        }

        // Hoisted, because three terms below want it now: the AO plane test, the sky rebuild and
        // the reflection weight. The split pass stores a unit vector, so this is a formality
        // against interpolation and a zero-length guard — worth doing once rather than three times.
        val n = Math.SafeNormalize(surface.xyz)

        val material = albedo.Sample(pointSampler, uv)
        var field = float2(1f, 1f)
        var contact = 1f

        if (useBilateral > 0f) {
            // The pixel's own surface, reconstructed the way the AO passes reconstructed theirs, so
            // the plane test below compares like with like.
            val deviceDepth = depthBuffer.Sample(pointSampler, uv).r
            val world = Transform.UvDepthToWorld(inverseViewProjection, uv, deviceDepth)

            field = Upsample(occlusion, occlusionTexel, world, n)
            contact = Upsample(contactOcclusion, contactTexel, world, n).x
        } else {
            field = occlusion.Sample(linearSampler, uv).rg
            contact = contactOcclusion.Sample(linearSampler, uv).r
        }

        // ⚠ **The sky, where no screen plane answers — the term the split would otherwise drop.**
        // Over π, in the plane's own units: `IrradianceSh9` returns irradiance and the multiply
        // below is by albedo alone, so the Lambert `1/π` that `Ibl.Diffuse` applies for the unsplit
        // pass has to be applied here instead. Without it every ambient-lit surface is π times too
        // bright, which reads as a badly exposed frame rather than as a missing constant.
        val sky = Ibl.IrradianceSh9(n, environmentSh) * (Const.InvPi * ambientIntensity * useEnvironmentSh)

        // A lerp rather than a sum, and `useIrradiance` is only ever 0 or 1: a screen plane's
        // irradiance already contains what the sky sends — the gather's rays hit it and the field's
        // did — so adding this to it would count the sky twice. It is a fallback, not a term.
        val incoming = lerp(sky, irradiance.Sample(linearSampler, uv).rgb, useIrradiance)

        // The material's own occlusion rides in the albedo plane's alpha, so it multiplies here
        // exactly as `Ibl.Diffuse` multiplied it before the split — and the two screen terms
        // stack over it, each answering at a scale the other cannot.
        val open = material.a * lerp(1f, field.x, useOcclusion) * lerp(1f, contact, useContactOcclusion)
        val sun = lerp(1f, field.y, useOcclusion)

        var color = lit.rgb * sun + material.rgb * incoming * open * intensity

        // The traced plane, weighted by what this surface actually reflects rather than by whether
        // the trace answered — see the ⚠ at the head of this file for what blending by validity
        // alone did.
        //
        // ⚠ **Added, not lerped, and the two are not interchangeable once `f0` is real.** A lerp
        // takes `reflectance ×` the whole combined colour away to make room, which at a metal's
        // 0.55 is half of its *direct* sunlight; and it is only the right shape at all while the
        // term being replaced is still in `lit`. With `useReflections` on it is not: the split pass
        // held its specular ambient back, so this is the frame's only specular ambient and the
        // arithmetic is a sum. A miss — alpha zero — is then genuinely nothing, which is honest:
        // `ReflectionTrace` writes 1 at every pixel that has a surface, so the only misses are the
        // pixels this function already returned early for.
        val mirror = reflections.Sample(linearSampler, uv)
        val f0 = lerp(float3(0.04f, 0.04f, 0.04f), specular.Sample(pointSampler, uv).rgb, useSpecular)
        val reflectance = Reflectance(f0, n, surface.a)

        color += mirror.rgb * reflectance * saturate(mirror.a) * useReflections

        return float4(color, lit.a)
    }

    /// How much of the traced radiance this surface sends to the eye — the weight the reflections
    /// plane is blended by, and the whole of the ⚠ at the head of this file.
    ///
    /// The split-sum specular integral, exactly as `Ibl.Specular` applies it to a prefiltered cube:
    /// `f0 · dfg.x + dfg.y`, with `Ibl.EnvironmentDfg` answering for this roughness at this angle.
    /// Weighing the plane by *that* is what makes the addition read as *supplying* the pass's IBL
    /// specular from the trace — the two are the same term over the same integral, one filtered off
    /// a cube and one marched — rather than as painting radiance over a finished frame.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **Three channels, because `f0` is.** A metal's `f0` is its base colour and a tinted one at
    /// that: galvanised steel is not grey-neutral, and folding this to a scalar would put a metal's
    /// reflection back at the wrong hue after going to the trouble of a plane to carry it. Where
    /// the caller has no plane it passes a dielectric 0.04 in all three, which is the number this
    /// function assumed for every surface before the plane existed — a chrome pillar reflecting at
    /// four per cent instead of ninety, which is the direction it is safe to be wrong in.
    ///
    /// The view direction comes from the frame's own unprojection rather than from a camera
    /// position, and needs no depth to do it: a pixel's view ray is a line in world space, so
    /// unprojecting this pixel at two different device depths gives two points on it and their
    /// difference is the direction. Reverse-Z, so 1 is the near plane and the nearer of the two.
    func Reflectance(f0: float3, n: float3, perceptualRoughness: float): float3 {
        var NdotV = 1f

        if (useView > 0f) {
            val near = Transform.UvDepthToWorld(inverseViewProjection, uv, 1f)
            val along = Transform.UvDepthToWorld(inverseViewProjection, uv, 0.5f)

            NdotV = saturate(dot(n, Math.SafeNormalize(near - along)))
        }

        val dfg = Ibl.EnvironmentDfg(NdotV, perceptualRoughness)

        return saturate(f0 * dfg.x + float3(dfg.y, dfg.y, dfg.y))
    }

    /// A reduced plane's `rg` at this pixel: its four nearest texels, each weighted by bilinear
    /// coverage and kept only if the surface it measured lies on this pixel's plane —
    /// `ScreenProbeUpsample`'s tap rule, one lattice down.
    ///
    /// Each texel's surface is re-read rather than carried: the AO pass point-sampled the
    /// full-resolution depth at its own texel centre, so point-sampling the same buffer at the same
    /// centre recovers exactly the depth its answer stands on. A texel across a depth edge fails
    /// the plane test and drops out, the weights renormalise over what is left, and a pixel every
    /// texel rejects takes the plain linear blend — a smeared answer over a hole, the fallback the
    /// probe upsample also takes. A full-resolution plane degenerates to its own texel at weight
    /// one, so the path costs nothing wrong when a host runs AO at scale 1.
    func Upsample(plane: Texture2D, texel: float2, world: float3, n: float3): float2 {
        val continuous = uv / texel - 0.5f
        val anchor = floor(continuous)
        val fraction = continuous - anchor

        var kept = float2(0f, 0f)
        var keptWeight = 0f

        for (corner in 0 .. 3) {
            val ox = corner == 1 || corner == 3 ? 1f : 0f
            val oy = corner >= 2 ? 1f : 0f
            val coverage = (ox > 0f ? fraction.x : 1f - fraction.x) * (oy > 0f ? fraction.y : 1f - fraction.y)

            if (coverage > 0f) {
                val tapUv = (anchor + float2(ox, oy) + 0.5f) * texel

                if (Fullscreen.InBounds(tapUv)) {
                    val tapDepth = depthBuffer.Sample(pointSampler, tapUv).r

                    // ZERO is the sky, because depth is reversed — a texel that took no march has
                    // no surface to agree with.
                    if (tapDepth > 0f) {
                        val theirs = Transform.UvDepthToWorld(inverseViewProjection, tapUv, tapDepth)

                        if (abs(dot(world - theirs, n)) <= planeTolerance) {
                            kept += plane.Sample(pointSampler, tapUv).rg * coverage
                            keptWeight += coverage
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        if (keptWeight <= 0f) {
            return plane.Sample(linearSampler, uv).rg
        }

        return kept / keptWeight
    }
}
